WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.178 --> 00:00:08.208
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.208 --> 00:00:10.158
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.158 --> 00:00:13.088
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:13.108 --> 00:00:14.918
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.918 --> 00:00:17.458
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.468 --> 00:00:19.438
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.438 --> 00:00:21.278
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.628 --> 00:00:24.708
I'm Alastair Budge, and today we are going

00:00:24.718 --> 00:00:27.728
to be talking about the history of fire.

00:00:28.378 --> 00:00:31.718
We humans are the only animal on Earth

00:00:32.008 --> 00:00:34.798
that has truly mastered fire: that has

00:00:34.798 --> 00:00:38.048
learned to keep it, make it, cook with it,

00:00:38.318 --> 00:00:41.228
and build entire civilisations around it.

00:00:41.588 --> 00:00:43.548
And today, we are going

00:00:43.548 --> 00:00:45.048
to trace that story.

00:00:45.288 --> 00:00:48.548
How fire changed our bodies, our minds,

00:00:48.778 --> 00:00:50.698
the way we interact with one another,

00:00:50.948 --> 00:00:53.678
and made the modern world possible.

00:00:54.678 --> 00:00:56.828
OK then, let's not waste a

00:00:56.838 --> 00:00:59.468
minute and get right into it.

00:01:00.948 --> 00:01:02.708
There is a cave in South

00:01:02.748 --> 00:01:04.848
Africa called Wonderwerk.

00:01:05.528 --> 00:01:09.127
The name means "miracle" in Afrikaans.

00:01:10.227 --> 00:01:13.978
This cave contains up to 7 metres of

00:01:13.978 --> 00:01:17.298
sediment, deposits, layers that had

00:01:17.358 --> 00:01:19.978
built up, millimetre by millimetre,

00:01:20.258 --> 00:01:23.428
over hundreds of thousands of years.

00:01:23.758 --> 00:01:26.118
Like a huge tower of human,

00:01:26.478 --> 00:01:28.508
and even pre-human, history.

00:01:29.858 --> 00:01:33.708
About one metre down, in a layer dating to

00:01:33.728 --> 00:01:37.238
roughly one million years ago, researchers

00:01:37.418 --> 00:01:40.378
from Boston University found charred

00:01:40.498 --> 00:01:44.178
plant material and burned bone fragments.

00:01:45.168 --> 00:01:47.908
Both had been heated to temperatures

00:01:48.138 --> 00:01:51.398
of around 500 degrees Celsius,

00:01:51.638 --> 00:01:53.778
almost 1,000 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:01:55.138 --> 00:01:57.138
This was not a wildfire

00:01:57.138 --> 00:01:58.258
that swept through.

00:01:58.868 --> 00:02:02.098
The burning had happened inside the cave,

00:02:02.418 --> 00:02:06.326
repeatedly, over time, in the same spot.

00:02:06.988 --> 00:02:08.538
It was the oldest known

00:02:08.588 --> 00:02:10.998
fireplace in the world.

00:02:12.208 --> 00:02:14.538
The creatures who most probably made

00:02:14.538 --> 00:02:17.248
this fire were called Homo erectus.

00:02:17.828 --> 00:02:20.288
That name, as the Latin scholars among

00:02:20.308 --> 00:02:23.918
you will know, simply means upright human.

00:02:24.458 --> 00:02:27.658
They were not Homo sapiens, not quite us.

00:02:27.998 --> 00:02:30.378
Their brains were smaller, their bones

00:02:30.418 --> 00:02:34.028
heavier, their foreheads thick and heavy.

00:02:35.028 --> 00:02:37.918
But they walked on two legs, they made

00:02:37.948 --> 00:02:41.168
stone tools, and, importantly for what

00:02:41.198 --> 00:02:44.858
we're talking about today, they used fire.

00:02:45.818 --> 00:02:49.158
Now, nobody knows exactly when the

00:02:49.168 --> 00:02:51.348
first of our ancestors started using

00:02:51.358 --> 00:02:54.418
fire; one million years ago is simply as

00:02:54.418 --> 00:02:57.088
far back as the reliable evidence goes

00:02:57.698 --> 00:02:59.728
What we do know is that

00:02:59.728 --> 00:03:02.068
for a very long period, our

00:03:02.108 --> 00:03:04.868
ancestors were not making fire.

00:03:05.348 --> 00:03:06.672
They were finding it.

00:03:07.658 --> 00:03:09.908
Fire breaks out in the wild, as

00:03:09.908 --> 00:03:11.628
it always has and always will.

00:03:12.168 --> 00:03:14.678
Lightning strikes a dry tree.

00:03:14.978 --> 00:03:17.979
A lava flow ignites grassland.

00:03:18.428 --> 00:03:20.508
Dry grass catches in the

00:03:20.518 --> 00:03:22.398
heat of a long summer.

00:03:23.378 --> 00:03:27.268
And almost every animal on Earth, when

00:03:27.268 --> 00:03:29.298
this happens, does the same thing.

00:03:30.148 --> 00:03:30.938
It runs.

00:03:31.388 --> 00:03:33.168
Fire is scary.

00:03:33.228 --> 00:03:33.958
It's hot.

00:03:34.658 --> 00:03:37.138
Instinct kicks in, and even

00:03:37.138 --> 00:03:39.158
the fiercest predator will

00:03:39.158 --> 00:03:41.448
run in the opposite direction.

00:03:42.738 --> 00:03:45.448
Homo erectus did something different.

00:03:46.298 --> 00:03:48.628
At some point, they approached.

00:03:49.818 --> 00:03:51.818
Perhaps they picked up a burning

00:03:51.818 --> 00:03:53.658
branch and carried it back.

00:03:54.118 --> 00:03:56.308
Perhaps they found smouldering

00:03:56.308 --> 00:03:58.178
wood and kept it alive.

00:03:59.008 --> 00:04:01.018
However it happened, the crucial

00:04:01.018 --> 00:04:04.208
step was this: they stopped merely

00:04:04.278 --> 00:04:07.509
fleeing fire and started managing it.

00:04:08.838 --> 00:04:11.628
Of course, this wasn't a simple task.

00:04:11.988 --> 00:04:13.518
As any seasoned camper will

00:04:13.518 --> 00:04:16.328
know, tending a fire and keeping

00:04:16.328 --> 00:04:18.968
it alight requires real effort.

00:04:19.288 --> 00:04:21.368
You have to find wood to burn,

00:04:21.778 --> 00:04:24.648
carry it, arrange it properly, and

00:04:24.678 --> 00:04:27.688
protect the flame from wind and rain.

00:04:28.208 --> 00:04:31.268
So keeping a fire alive required

00:04:31.318 --> 00:04:35.108
attention, planning, and cooperation.

00:04:35.207 --> 00:04:36.788
It required something that looks,

00:04:37.108 --> 00:04:39.982
in other words, very much like care.

00:04:41.038 --> 00:04:44.218
And as for why Homo erectus would

00:04:44.218 --> 00:04:46.765
want to have a fire, well, there

00:04:46.844 --> 00:04:49.217
are numerous immediate benefits.

00:04:49.508 --> 00:04:51.608
Warmth through cold nights.

00:04:51.938 --> 00:04:53.198
Light to see by.

00:04:53.588 --> 00:04:56.588
And perhaps most importantly, safety.

00:04:57.458 --> 00:05:00.259
A fire keeps large predators away.

00:05:00.971 --> 00:05:03.708
And in a world where leopards,

00:05:03.858 --> 00:05:06.538
lions, hyenas, and sabre-toothed

00:05:06.538 --> 00:05:09.058
cats were a constant menace,

00:05:09.298 --> 00:05:11.737
especially in the darkness of night,

00:05:12.278 --> 00:05:14.608
fire was a wonderful deterrent.

00:05:15.618 --> 00:05:18.888
But fire changed humans in ways that

00:05:18.888 --> 00:05:22.198
go far deeper than warmth and safety.

00:05:23.368 --> 00:05:27.838
In 2009, a Harvard professor named

00:05:27.868 --> 00:05:31.178
Richard Wrangham published a book called

00:05:31.498 --> 00:05:35.528
Catching Fire: How Cooking Made Us Human.

00:05:36.728 --> 00:05:38.928
The argument, as you might guess

00:05:38.938 --> 00:05:41.568
from the title, is that cooking food

00:05:41.798 --> 00:05:44.708
rather than eating it raw is one of

00:05:44.708 --> 00:05:47.468
the fundamental differences between

00:05:47.488 --> 00:05:50.128
humans and every other animal.

00:05:51.208 --> 00:05:54.218
And the consequences go far deeper

00:05:54.668 --> 00:05:56.228
than simply getting to enjoy a

00:05:56.238 --> 00:05:59.338
hotter, and dare I say, tastier meal.

00:06:00.588 --> 00:06:02.488
His argument is this.

00:06:03.348 --> 00:06:06.368
Raw food is hard to digest.

00:06:06.978 --> 00:06:10.238
Your body has to work extremely hard

00:06:10.458 --> 00:06:13.153
to extract calories from uncooked

00:06:13.272 --> 00:06:16.108
meats, roots, and plant material.

00:06:17.448 --> 00:06:19.788
Cooking changes all of that.

00:06:20.348 --> 00:06:22.958
It breaks down the cellular structure of

00:06:22.958 --> 00:06:25.778
food before it even enters your mouth.

00:06:26.288 --> 00:06:28.068
Proteins unravel.

00:06:28.498 --> 00:06:31.378
Starches become easier to absorb.

00:06:31.938 --> 00:06:33.978
Tough fibres soften.

00:06:35.358 --> 00:06:38.338
And cooked food releases anywhere

00:06:38.338 --> 00:06:41.188
between 30 and 100 percent more

00:06:41.188 --> 00:06:44.668
calories than the same food eaten raw.

00:06:45.389 --> 00:06:48.236
So if you are getting more energy

00:06:48.596 --> 00:06:51.206
from the same amount of food, you do

00:06:51.206 --> 00:06:53.866
not need such a large, energy-hungry

00:06:53.896 --> 00:06:55.956
digestive system to process it.

00:06:56.746 --> 00:06:59.066
And the energy you are no longer

00:06:59.066 --> 00:07:02.016
spending on digestion becomes

00:07:02.016 --> 00:07:04.086
available for something else.

00:07:05.156 --> 00:07:07.306
Wrangham's argument is that this

00:07:07.336 --> 00:07:10.496
surplus energy went to the brain.

00:07:11.646 --> 00:07:13.866
He points to Homo erectus, who

00:07:13.896 --> 00:07:17.796
appeared roughly 1.8 million years ago.

00:07:18.882 --> 00:07:21.998
Earlier hominins, like Australopithecus,

00:07:22.036 --> 00:07:26.286
had large guts, small brains, and big

00:07:26.316 --> 00:07:31.026
teeth suited to chewing tough, raw food.

00:07:31.936 --> 00:07:34.776
Homo erectus had dramatically

00:07:34.796 --> 00:07:37.846
smaller teeth and a smaller gut.

00:07:38.606 --> 00:07:41.956
But a noticeably larger brain.

00:07:43.016 --> 00:07:46.466
This shift happens at roughly the time

00:07:46.686 --> 00:07:49.736
we see the first signs of fire use.

00:07:51.064 --> 00:07:52.726
Wrangham's claim is that

00:07:52.736 --> 00:07:54.786
this is not a coincidence.

00:07:55.456 --> 00:07:58.346
Cooking made this transition possible.

00:07:58.936 --> 00:08:01.396
Perhaps cooking drove it.

00:08:02.526 --> 00:08:04.726
Now, not everyone agrees.

00:08:05.256 --> 00:08:07.726
Some researchers argue the energy

00:08:07.726 --> 00:08:10.236
came from raw meat and bone marrow.

00:08:10.746 --> 00:08:12.616
Their argument is that eating

00:08:12.626 --> 00:08:15.196
more animal protein, not cooking,

00:08:15.456 --> 00:08:17.606
explains the anatomical changes.

00:08:18.166 --> 00:08:21.026
But here is what is not in

00:08:21.026 --> 00:08:23.906
dispute: we are the only animal

00:08:23.966 --> 00:08:26.526
on earth that cooks its food.

00:08:27.646 --> 00:08:29.246
Chimpanzees are our

00:08:29.286 --> 00:08:30.896
closest living relatives.

00:08:31.206 --> 00:08:35.042
They share roughly 98 percent of our DNA.

00:08:35.536 --> 00:08:37.916
And they have never, in any

00:08:37.966 --> 00:08:40.386
documented setting, cooked anything.

00:08:41.556 --> 00:08:43.786
And it is not because they don't want to.

00:08:44.646 --> 00:08:47.156
In a series of experiments by

00:08:47.176 --> 00:08:49.566
researchers at Harvard and Yale,

00:08:50.226 --> 00:08:53.156
chimpanzees were given a choice between

00:08:53.176 --> 00:08:56.306
a raw piece of food and a cooked one.

00:08:57.416 --> 00:08:59.546
All 29 chimpanzees in the

00:08:59.556 --> 00:09:02.205
study chose the cooked option.

00:09:03.246 --> 00:09:06.365
And more strikingly: when the chimps

00:09:06.405 --> 00:09:07.756
were shown that they could place

00:09:08.006 --> 00:09:10.886
a raw piece of food into a device

00:09:11.166 --> 00:09:13.176
that would produce a cooked version

00:09:13.296 --> 00:09:16.556
afterwards, they were willing to wait.

00:09:17.466 --> 00:09:19.656
So even though they wanted to

00:09:19.706 --> 00:09:22.896
eat, they held back from eating

00:09:22.896 --> 00:09:25.986
the raw food, specifically in

00:09:26.036 --> 00:09:27.896
order to get the cooked version.

00:09:28.566 --> 00:09:31.436
So chimpanzees understand the

00:09:31.476 --> 00:09:35.376
transformation, from raw to cooked food.

00:09:35.796 --> 00:09:37.316
They prefer the result.

00:09:37.776 --> 00:09:39.496
They will even delay

00:09:39.526 --> 00:09:41.369
gratification to get it.

00:09:41.846 --> 00:09:42.736
They just don't know

00:09:42.956 --> 00:09:45.196
how to use or make fire.

00:09:46.326 --> 00:09:47.396
Humans do.

00:09:47.846 --> 00:09:50.456
And that single difference may

00:09:50.476 --> 00:09:52.866
be the most consequential in the

00:09:52.876 --> 00:09:55.566
entire history of life on Earth.

00:09:56.716 --> 00:09:59.306
Now, there is one more physical

00:09:59.346 --> 00:10:01.766
consequence of fire we need to talk about.

00:10:02.286 --> 00:10:03.956
It concerns sleep.

00:10:05.196 --> 00:10:07.576
Before fire, our ancestors

00:10:07.876 --> 00:10:10.366
almost certainly slept in trees,

00:10:10.656 --> 00:10:13.136
as most great apes still do.

00:10:13.866 --> 00:10:16.366
Trees are safer from predators.

00:10:16.836 --> 00:10:18.546
But you don't have to have slept

00:10:18.566 --> 00:10:20.576
in many trees yourself to know

00:10:20.576 --> 00:10:22.626
that trees are not particularly

00:10:22.666 --> 00:10:24.836
comfortable places to spend the night.

00:10:25.346 --> 00:10:27.746
You wake up frequently, you

00:10:27.746 --> 00:10:30.223
sleep lightly and uncomfortably.

00:10:31.066 --> 00:10:33.776
Chimpanzees sleep around ten

00:10:33.806 --> 00:10:35.336
hours a night because their

00:10:35.336 --> 00:10:37.636
sleep is so frequently disturbed.

00:10:38.356 --> 00:10:41.736
Humans sleep seven to eight hours.

00:10:42.156 --> 00:10:44.216
And we sleep deeply.

00:10:45.366 --> 00:10:48.596
And to bring this back to fire, with

00:10:48.596 --> 00:10:51.266
a fire burning through the night, you

00:10:51.266 --> 00:10:54.266
could sleep on the ground and be safe.

00:10:55.076 --> 00:10:57.196
And because you felt safe, and you

00:10:57.196 --> 00:10:59.646
were safe, you could sleep properly.

00:11:00.736 --> 00:11:03.156
The deep, dreaming sleep that

00:11:03.166 --> 00:11:05.826
consolidates memory, repairs the body,

00:11:06.176 --> 00:11:08.786
and allows the brain to do its work.

00:11:09.666 --> 00:11:12.646
The depth of human sleep, tied to

00:11:12.866 --> 00:11:15.906
memory, learning, creativity, and

00:11:15.916 --> 00:11:19.646
health, may itself be, in part, a

00:11:19.686 --> 00:11:22.806
consequence of learning to manage fire.

00:11:24.156 --> 00:11:27.596
And by moving our beds from the

00:11:27.606 --> 00:11:30.316
branches to the ground, we created

00:11:30.346 --> 00:11:33.586
a new physical space: the circle.

00:11:34.516 --> 00:11:37.886
And it was in this circle, protected

00:11:37.896 --> 00:11:40.056
by the flames, that the second

00:11:40.076 --> 00:11:42.706
great transformation happened: we

00:11:42.706 --> 00:11:45.786
started to talk in a different way.

00:11:47.116 --> 00:11:49.266
An anthropologist at the University of

00:11:49.306 --> 00:11:53.346
Utah named Polly Wiessner spent four

00:11:53.346 --> 00:11:56.556
decades studying a group of Kalahari

00:11:56.556 --> 00:11:59.016
Desert bushmen in southern Africa.

00:12:00.266 --> 00:12:03.386
This group is one of the oldest-surviving

00:12:03.396 --> 00:12:05.916
hunter-gatherer cultures on earth.

00:12:06.466 --> 00:12:08.296
She analysed hundreds

00:12:08.306 --> 00:12:09.706
of their conversations.

00:12:10.226 --> 00:12:13.226
And she found a very clear pattern.

00:12:14.196 --> 00:12:16.676
During the day, conversations

00:12:16.916 --> 00:12:18.566
were mostly practical.

00:12:19.326 --> 00:12:20.556
Economic decisions.

00:12:20.986 --> 00:12:21.636
Disputes.

00:12:21.976 --> 00:12:22.656
Complaints.

00:12:23.046 --> 00:12:26.076
Discussions about food and hunting.

00:12:26.986 --> 00:12:29.166
Around the fire at night, the

00:12:29.176 --> 00:12:31.312
topics were very different.

00:12:31.976 --> 00:12:35.076
Storytelling accounted for 81 percent

00:12:35.256 --> 00:12:37.756
of all conversations, compared

00:12:37.956 --> 00:12:40.566
to only 6 percent during the day.

00:12:41.336 --> 00:12:44.876
At night, by firelight, people told

00:12:44.876 --> 00:12:47.916
stories about distant lands, about people

00:12:48.266 --> 00:12:50.566
they had never met, about ancestors

00:12:50.756 --> 00:12:53.536
and spirits and the distant past.

00:12:54.046 --> 00:12:55.616
They speculated about

00:12:55.616 --> 00:12:57.466
things they could not see.

00:12:57.746 --> 00:12:59.106
They laughed.

00:12:59.336 --> 00:13:00.096
They imagined.

00:13:01.186 --> 00:13:05.466
Many researchers argue this is where human

00:13:05.466 --> 00:13:08.876
language, storytelling, and imagination

00:13:09.196 --> 00:13:12.676
really grew, around the calm of the fire.

00:13:13.396 --> 00:13:15.816
Stories bind communities.

00:13:16.006 --> 00:13:19.036
They carry knowledge across generations.

00:13:19.376 --> 00:13:21.616
They let us think about distant

00:13:21.656 --> 00:13:24.946
places and people, past events, and

00:13:24.946 --> 00:13:27.356
futures that have not yet happened.

00:13:28.846 --> 00:13:31.476
And the fire restructured domestic

00:13:31.476 --> 00:13:33.526
life in practical ways too.

00:13:34.456 --> 00:13:37.176
Cooking with fire requires different

00:13:37.206 --> 00:13:39.716
tasks: collecting the wood, starting

00:13:39.716 --> 00:13:41.816
the fire, tending the fire, doing the

00:13:41.826 --> 00:13:44.636
cooking, and, of course, someone still

00:13:44.636 --> 00:13:47.226
needs to go and hunt the unfortunate

00:13:47.266 --> 00:13:49.126
animal everyone is going to eat.

00:13:49.916 --> 00:13:51.866
This requires co-operation,

00:13:52.276 --> 00:13:55.216
specialisation, and division of labour.

00:13:56.096 --> 00:13:59.136
Often, though not always, cooking

00:13:59.176 --> 00:14:01.036
became associated more with

00:14:01.036 --> 00:14:03.616
women, and hunting more with men.

00:14:04.546 --> 00:14:07.106
And Wrangham, in the book I

00:14:07.106 --> 00:14:09.266
mentioned earlier, makes another,

00:14:09.336 --> 00:14:11.346
more provocative suggestion.

00:14:12.396 --> 00:14:15.736
He argues that cooking may have been one

00:14:15.736 --> 00:14:19.091
of the early drivers of pair bonding,

00:14:19.304 --> 00:14:22.086
of a more stable relationship between

00:14:22.086 --> 00:14:25.436
a man and a woman, based less on

00:14:25.436 --> 00:14:28.806
romantic love than on mutual advantage.

00:14:29.686 --> 00:14:32.656
Imagine a woman crouching over a fire,

00:14:32.906 --> 00:14:34.626
with cooked food in front of her.

00:14:35.656 --> 00:14:37.846
That food was valuable.

00:14:38.306 --> 00:14:40.064
It had taken time to prepare.

00:14:40.626 --> 00:14:44.016
And because it was soft, warm, and

00:14:44.026 --> 00:14:47.156
ready to eat, it could also be stolen.

00:14:48.386 --> 00:14:50.716
Wrangham’s argument is that a man

00:14:50.956 --> 00:14:53.656
could provide protection, especially

00:14:53.906 --> 00:14:56.346
from other members of the group, in

00:14:56.346 --> 00:15:00.016
exchange for a share of that cooked food.

00:15:01.006 --> 00:15:04.466
Over time, he suggests, this practical

00:15:04.466 --> 00:15:06.616
arrangement may have helped shape

00:15:06.616 --> 00:15:09.636
something closer to human pair bonding:

00:15:10.126 --> 00:15:13.126
not marriage as we understand it today,

00:15:13.716 --> 00:15:17.286
but an early form of domestic partnership.

00:15:18.216 --> 00:15:20.376
It is, I should stress, simply

00:15:20.396 --> 00:15:22.926
a theory, but an interesting one

00:15:22.996 --> 00:15:25.236
nonetheless, that the first human

00:15:25.236 --> 00:15:28.576
couples got together and stayed together

00:15:28.966 --> 00:15:31.236
in order to protect their dinner.

00:15:32.226 --> 00:15:34.976
So, for hundreds of thousands of years,

00:15:34.976 --> 00:15:38.626
then, early humans found fire, kept

00:15:38.626 --> 00:15:41.266
it, and built their lives around it.

00:15:41.856 --> 00:15:44.506
But, importantly, they were

00:15:44.556 --> 00:15:48.146
entirely at the mercy of finding it.

00:15:48.646 --> 00:15:51.046
If the flame went out, that was it.

00:15:51.486 --> 00:15:52.916
You had to wait for lightning

00:15:52.916 --> 00:15:55.186
to strike again, or travel far

00:15:55.366 --> 00:15:57.286
to find fire somewhere else.

00:15:57.806 --> 00:16:01.356
The warmth, the safety, the cooked food,

00:16:01.366 --> 00:16:04.946
the community — all of it depended on

00:16:04.946 --> 00:16:08.586
a flame that someone, somewhere, had

00:16:08.586 --> 00:16:10.646
to have been lucky enough to find.

00:16:11.886 --> 00:16:14.206
That dependence is what changed

00:16:14.406 --> 00:16:16.796
around 400,000 years ago.

00:16:17.516 --> 00:16:19.976
We stopped waiting for the lightning,

00:16:20.326 --> 00:16:23.166
and learned how to make fire ourselves.

00:16:24.366 --> 00:16:26.386
First, it's thought it was through

00:16:26.386 --> 00:16:29.486
percussion, striking pieces of flint

00:16:29.756 --> 00:16:33.376
against iron pyrite to create sparks that

00:16:33.376 --> 00:16:35.866
could catch in a small bundle of tinder:

00:16:36.346 --> 00:16:39.236
small, dry pieces of wood or leaves.

00:16:39.966 --> 00:16:43.156
Later came the friction method: spinning

00:16:43.176 --> 00:16:46.536
a dry wooden rod against a flat piece

00:16:46.556 --> 00:16:49.496
of softer wood until there's enough heat

00:16:49.526 --> 00:16:52.896
built up to set dry leaves or wood alight.

00:16:53.686 --> 00:16:56.086
There is of course no way to know

00:16:56.256 --> 00:16:57.786
what it felt like when the first

00:16:57.856 --> 00:17:00.596
person, wherever he or she was,

00:17:00.996 --> 00:17:02.826
figured out how to create fire.

00:17:03.386 --> 00:17:06.316
But it must have felt like magic, like

00:17:06.566 --> 00:17:08.776
you had just figured out the secret to

00:17:08.776 --> 00:17:10.976
one of the world's greatest puzzles.

00:17:11.896 --> 00:17:13.556
And they had, really.

00:17:14.576 --> 00:17:17.606
But for most of the million-year

00:17:17.616 --> 00:17:21.206
story of fire, the fuel was simple.

00:17:21.666 --> 00:17:23.776
It was wood, for the most part.

00:17:24.286 --> 00:17:26.756
Sometimes dried grass, peat,

00:17:27.046 --> 00:17:28.626
charcoal, or animal dung.

00:17:29.106 --> 00:17:31.506
Things that had grown under the sun,

00:17:31.906 --> 00:17:34.956
stored its energy, and released it again

00:17:35.196 --> 00:17:37.666
as heat and light when they burned.

00:17:38.606 --> 00:17:40.426
And it was enough.

00:17:40.826 --> 00:17:42.976
Enough to build every civilisation

00:17:43.306 --> 00:17:45.706
before roughly 300 years ago.

00:17:46.396 --> 00:17:49.606
The ancient Egyptians, classical Greece,

00:17:49.646 --> 00:17:52.876
the Roman Empire, medieval Europe: all

00:17:52.916 --> 00:17:56.336
of it ran on burning wood and charcoal.

00:17:56.826 --> 00:17:59.876
You could smelt metals, fire pottery,

00:18:00.016 --> 00:18:03.936
heat homes, cook food, and light streets.

00:18:04.996 --> 00:18:07.436
Then, gradually, the

00:18:07.436 --> 00:18:09.266
wood started to run out.

00:18:10.446 --> 00:18:13.416
In Britain, by the 17th century,

00:18:13.866 --> 00:18:16.066
forests had been stripped.

00:18:16.666 --> 00:18:19.766
Wood for building ships, for building

00:18:19.766 --> 00:18:23.796
houses, for heating: all of it was scarce

00:18:24.066 --> 00:18:26.316
and getting increasingly expensive.

00:18:26.786 --> 00:18:29.106
But people still needed fire, or

00:18:29.106 --> 00:18:31.536
rather, the heat that fire gave out.

00:18:32.486 --> 00:18:35.016
The answer was right under their

00:18:35.026 --> 00:18:38.156
noses, or to be precise, under

00:18:38.156 --> 00:18:41.766
their feet: coal, and later, oil.

00:18:42.976 --> 00:18:46.276
Now, the existence of coal had been

00:18:46.316 --> 00:18:48.436
common knowledge for centuries.

00:18:48.726 --> 00:18:50.946
It washed up on the beach, and

00:18:51.126 --> 00:18:52.836
you could collect it in small

00:18:52.866 --> 00:18:55.846
quantities from hillsides, or from

00:18:55.856 --> 00:18:57.596
relatively close to the surface.

00:18:58.286 --> 00:19:01.176
And people in northeast England and

00:19:01.176 --> 00:19:03.646
South Wales had been burning it in

00:19:03.646 --> 00:19:05.856
small quantities for generations.

00:19:06.416 --> 00:19:09.066
But it was regarded as a poor

00:19:09.116 --> 00:19:12.806
substitute: dirtier, smellier, and

00:19:12.836 --> 00:19:14.896
compared to wood, which you just got

00:19:14.896 --> 00:19:17.296
from trees, it was much harder to get

00:19:17.296 --> 00:19:19.936
at in any sort of useful quantity.

00:19:20.956 --> 00:19:23.356
But there wasn't an alternative;

00:19:23.906 --> 00:19:26.136
England was running out of trees,

00:19:26.346 --> 00:19:28.336
it was running out of wood.

00:19:29.626 --> 00:19:33.346
And this need, this desperation, was

00:19:33.346 --> 00:19:36.086
the first domino in the Industrial

00:19:36.086 --> 00:19:39.126
Revolution, and you could even argue

00:19:39.576 --> 00:19:41.256
every technological development

00:19:41.436 --> 00:19:44.016
that you and I benefit from today.

00:19:45.370 --> 00:19:49.790
In 1709, a man named Abraham Darby

00:19:50.080 --> 00:19:54.050
worked out how to make iron using coke,

00:19:54.380 --> 00:19:57.930
a purified, high-carbon form of coal.

00:19:59.140 --> 00:20:01.290
This was the first time someone

00:20:01.290 --> 00:20:03.490
had managed to make iron with coal,

00:20:03.730 --> 00:20:06.423
rather than charcoal, burnt wood.

00:20:08.017 --> 00:20:10.697
And this had considerable advantages.

00:20:11.577 --> 00:20:13.947
Using coal was much cheaper.

00:20:14.527 --> 00:20:16.947
It turned iron, and later steel,

00:20:17.187 --> 00:20:19.847
from scarce, expensive materials

00:20:20.087 --> 00:20:22.057
into industrial commodities.

00:20:23.057 --> 00:20:25.957
And more importantly, coal

00:20:26.237 --> 00:20:29.287
appeared to be virtually limitless.

00:20:30.307 --> 00:20:32.497
Before this, iron production

00:20:32.737 --> 00:20:34.487
was limited by the rate at

00:20:34.487 --> 00:20:36.477
which forests could grow back.

00:20:37.607 --> 00:20:39.887
After Darby, it was limited

00:20:40.187 --> 00:20:42.657
only by how fast you could dig.

00:20:43.517 --> 00:20:45.947
And Britain dug very fast.

00:20:46.587 --> 00:20:49.817
In 1700, roughly five-sixths

00:20:50.027 --> 00:20:52.607
of all coal mined in the

00:20:52.607 --> 00:20:55.177
entire world came from Britain.

00:20:56.257 --> 00:20:59.237
Annual iron output, which stood at

00:20:59.257 --> 00:21:03.724
around 2,500 tons in the early 1700s,

00:21:03.877 --> 00:21:08.375
reached 2.5 million tons by 1850.

00:21:08.747 --> 00:21:11.357
A thousand-fold increase in

00:21:11.357 --> 00:21:13.017
just a century and a half.

00:21:14.027 --> 00:21:16.327
Now, I don't want to turn this into an

00:21:16.337 --> 00:21:18.507
episode about the Industrial Revolution

00:21:18.867 --> 00:21:21.077
[we already have one on that, it's episode

00:21:21.077 --> 00:21:25.187
number 150], but the main point is that

00:21:25.197 --> 00:21:28.687
this was primarily a fire revolution.

00:21:29.407 --> 00:21:32.467
It was about inventing better and better

00:21:32.467 --> 00:21:35.557
ways to create energy, to create heat,

00:21:35.907 --> 00:21:38.557
and then transform that into different

00:21:38.557 --> 00:21:41.867
forms of energy and industrial output.

00:21:43.267 --> 00:21:46.467
And after that, came oil, which is an

00:21:46.567 --> 00:21:49.927
even more energy-dense fuel than coal, and

00:21:49.947 --> 00:21:53.017
being liquid, is far easier to transport.

00:21:53.937 --> 00:21:55.977
What followed was the fastest

00:21:56.007 --> 00:21:58.307
transformation of human life in the

00:21:58.317 --> 00:22:01.097
entire million-year story of fire.

00:22:01.677 --> 00:22:05.247
Cars, aeroplanes, ships, factories,

00:22:05.437 --> 00:22:08.247
central heating, electric light: all

00:22:08.277 --> 00:22:11.407
of it, at its root, a consequence of

00:22:11.417 --> 00:22:14.567
setting fire to stuff and using its heat.

00:22:15.767 --> 00:22:18.697
Now, it would be remiss of me not to

00:22:18.697 --> 00:22:20.757
mention the environmental side-effects

00:22:20.757 --> 00:22:23.567
of all of this, the trillions of tonnes

00:22:23.597 --> 00:22:26.237
of CO2 this is all estimated to have

00:22:26.237 --> 00:22:28.747
released into the atmosphere, and what

00:22:28.757 --> 00:22:31.017
this might mean for the planet's future.

00:22:31.927 --> 00:22:34.137
But to come back to where we started.

00:22:34.787 --> 00:22:36.837
Sometime around a million years

00:22:36.837 --> 00:22:39.977
ago, our early ancestors found fire

00:22:39.987 --> 00:22:42.177
in the wild and kept it burning.

00:22:42.737 --> 00:22:45.447
Then we learned how to make it ourselves.

00:22:45.927 --> 00:22:48.537
We used it to cook our food, shape

00:22:48.577 --> 00:22:51.157
our societies, and build things our

00:22:51.207 --> 00:22:53.427
ancestors could never have imagined.

00:22:54.207 --> 00:22:56.577
Fire might seem like just another

00:22:56.577 --> 00:22:58.087
part of the natural world.

00:22:58.503 --> 00:23:01.937
But for us humans, it changed everything.

00:23:03.560 --> 00:23:05.670
OK then, that is it for today's

00:23:05.690 --> 00:23:08.225
episode on the history of fire.

00:23:08.570 --> 00:23:10.000
I hope it's been an interesting one,

00:23:10.240 --> 00:23:11.990
and that you've learnt something new.

00:23:12.330 --> 00:23:14.060
As always, I would love to know

00:23:14.290 --> 00:23:15.880
what you thought of this episode.

00:23:16.100 --> 00:23:17.040
Let me know in the comments

00:23:17.040 --> 00:23:18.220
below, if you're listening

00:23:18.240 --> 00:23:19.560
somewhere where you can comment.

00:23:19.860 --> 00:23:21.820
And for the members among you, you can

00:23:21.820 --> 00:23:23.930
head right into our community forum at

00:23:23.930 --> 00:23:26.960
community.leonardoenglish.com and get

00:23:26.960 --> 00:23:29.120
chatting away to other curious minds.

00:23:29.528 --> 00:23:31.490
,     You've been listening to English Learning

00:23:31.490 --> 00:23:34.250
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:23:34.630 --> 00:23:37.610
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:23:37.610 --> 00:23:39.790
I'll catch you in the next episode.

